<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net" modified="2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z" agent="Trae"><diagram id="pipeline" name="Pipeline"><mxGraphModel dx="1800" dy="2600" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="2200" pageHeight="3200" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0" /><mxCell id="1" parent="0" /><mxCell id="header" value="SciAtlas Idea Generation Pipeline&#10;Run dir: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/chemstry_question/06-&#10;塑料废物管理/full&#10;Topic: Research question: How can we better manage the&#10;world’s plastic waste?&#10;Context: One of the challenges of managing plastic waste&#10;is a lack of reliable data: The complicated global streams&#10;that produce and distribute waste from petroleum-based&#10;products are not always clear. Recently, experts in&#10;management and sustainability collaborated to address this&#10;issue by focusing on material flow analysis in Trinidad&#10;and Tobago, an island nation of 1.2 million people.&#10;Correlating publicly available data, researchers&#10;investigated the origins of waste and surmised that&#10;recycling plastic bottles and banning plastic bags would&#10;be economically feasible and environmentally advantageous,&#10;and also that plastics could be used as fuel in cement&#10;plants. They concluded that material flow analysis is&#10;useful for such efforts.&#10;Status: ok&#10;Failed step: N/A" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=14;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="40" width="442" height="456" /></mxCell><mxCell id="params" value="Input Parameters&#10;topic: Research question: How can we better manage the&#10;world’s plastic waste?&#10;Context: One of the challenges of managing plastic waste&#10;is a lack of reliable data: The complicated global streams&#10;that produce and distribute waste from petroleum-based&#10;products are not always clear. Recently, experts in&#10;management and sustainability collaborated to address this&#10;issue by focusing on material flow analysis in Trinidad&#10;and Tobago, an island nation of 1.2 million people.&#10;Correlating publicly available data, researchers&#10;investigated the origins of waste and surmised that&#10;recycling plastic bottles and banning plastic bags would&#10;be economically feasible and environmentally advantageous,&#10;and also that plastics could be used as fuel in cement&#10;plants. They concluded that material flow analysis is&#10;useful for such efforts.&#10;domain: Chemistry&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;&#10;Effective Pipeline Config&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;anchor_top_k: 5&#10;keyword_min_papers: 2&#10;k_step1: 4&#10;seed_num_queries: 8&#10;seed_per_query: 3&#10;seed_request_multiplier: 6&#10;seed_request_floor: 12&#10;seed_recent_years: 7&#10;seed_min_citations: 1000&#10;graph_budget_ratio: 0.35&#10;graph_budget_min: 12&#10;graph_budget_max: 30&#10;graph_max_predecessors_per_paper: 3&#10;graph_min_forward_papers: 3&#10;graph_candidate_fetch_limit: 40&#10;num_candidate_step2: 3&#10;num_expansion_step2: 1&#10;innoeval_root: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/SciAtlas/referen&#10;ces/search/src/innoeval_search&#10;grobid_base_url: http://127.0.0.1:8070&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_same_field: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;max_novelty_feedback_rounds: 2&#10;idea_count: 1&#10;runs_dir: runs&#10;&#10;Effective SciAtlas Config&#10;base_url: http://sciatlas.openkg.cn&#10;api_key: ***REDACTED***&#10;timeout: 900&#10;max_retries: 4&#10;retry_backoff: 5.0&#10;use_cache: True&#10;cache_dir: runs/cache&#10;official_cli_root: .&#10;use_local_kg: False&#10;local_search_root: references/search" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="40" width="442" height="1176" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step1" value="Step 1 Anchor Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;seed_paper_count: 4" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="340" width="220" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step2" value="Step 2 Research Graph Construction&#10;status: completed&#10;graph_paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="500" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step1_step_step2" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step1" target="step_step2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step2_graph_title" value="Step 2 Annotated Research Graph&#10;paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0&#10;phase_count: 1&#10;directions: anchor=4, backward=0,&#10;forward=0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=13;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="500" width="267" height="132" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_title" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step2_graph_title"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed" value="Seed Paper 1/4&#10;Online Characterization of Mixed&#10;Plastic Waste Using Machine&#10;Learning and Mid-Infrared&#10;Spectroscopy&#10;year: 2022&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4309475252&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="672" width="260" height="180" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_0" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_1" value="Seed Paper 2/4&#10;DETECTING CONTAMINANTS IN POST-&#10;CONSUMER PLASTIC PACKAGING WASTE&#10;BY A NIR HYPERSPECTRAL IMAGING-&#10;BASED CASCADE DETECTION APPROACH&#10;year: 2021&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W3176826653&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="640" y="672" width="260" height="180" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_1" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_2" value="Seed Paper 3/4&#10;Identification of Aged&#10;Polypropylene with Machine&#10;Learning and Near–Infrared&#10;Spectroscopy for Improved&#10;Recycling&#10;year: 2025&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4408188181&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="672" width="260" height="196" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_2" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_3" value="Seed Paper 4/4&#10;Development of an Economically&#10;Viable AI-Enabled Sorting&#10;Technology for Complex Plastic&#10;Waste Streams&#10;year: 2025&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W7128697218&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="1200" y="672" width="260" height="180" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_3" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step3" value="Step 3 Graph Serialization + Trend&#10;status: completed&#10;serialized_chars: 6307&#10;trend_chars: 7737" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="968" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step2_step_step3" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step_step3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step3_panel" value="Step 3 Serialization + Trend&#10;Serialized Graph:&#10;# SEED META topic: Research question: How can we better manage&#10;the world’s plastic waste? Context: One of the challenges of&#10;managing plastic waste is a lack of reliable data: The&#10;complicated global streams that produce and distribute waste&#10;from petroleum-based products are not always clear. Recently,&#10;experts in management and sustainability collaborated to&#10;address this issue by focusing on material flow analysis in&#10;Trinidad and Tobago, an island nation of 1.2 million people.&#10;Correlating publicly available data, researchers investigated&#10;the origins of waste and surmised that recycling plastic&#10;bottles and banning plastic bags would be economically&#10;feasible and environmentally advantageous, and also that&#10;plastics could be used as fuel in cement plants. They&#10;concluded that material flow analysis is useful for such&#10;efforts. seed_paper: Online Characterization of Mixed Plastic&#10;Waste Using Machine Learning and Mid-Infrared Spectroscopy #&#10;CITATION SUBGRAPH (3 refs, temporally ordered by year) ## [1]&#10;DETECTING CONTAMINANTS IN POST-CONSUMER PLASTIC PACKAGING&#10;WASTE BY A NIR HYPERSPECTRAL IMAGING-BASED CASCADE DETECTION&#10;APPROACH (2021, venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth = 1&#10;cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 16 section_weight = 0.0&#10;delta_year = unknown is_influential = False low_confidence =&#10;True cited_by_subgraph = 0 [PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[]&#10;delta_yr=unknown edge_type=none [IDEA] Problem: Recycling of&#10;post-consumer packaging wastes involves a complex chain of&#10;activities, usually based on three main stages, that is: i)&#10;collection from households or recovery from Municipal solid&#10;waste (MSW), ii) sorting and, finally, iii) mechanical&#10;recycling. The systematic identification of impuritie Existing&#10;Methods: Unavailable from local metadata. Motivation:&#10;Recycling of post-consumer packaging wastes involves a complex&#10;chain of activities, usually based on three main stages, that&#10;is: i) collection from households or recovery from Municipal&#10;solid waste (MSW), ii) sorting and, finally, iii) mechanical&#10;recycling. The systematic identification of impuritie Proposed&#10;Method: Recycling of post-consumer packaging wastes involves a&#10;complex chain of activities, usually based on three main&#10;stages, that is: i) collection from households or recovery&#10;from Municipal solid waste (MSW), ii) sorting and, finally,&#10;iii) mechanical recycling. The systematic identification of&#10;impuritie Experiment Plan: Unavailable from local metadata.&#10;[ABSTRACT] Recycling of post-consumer packaging wastes&#10;involves a complex chain of activities, usually based on three&#10;main stages, that is: i) collection from households or&#10;recovery from Municipal solid waste (MSW), ii) sorting and,&#10;finally, iii) mechanical recycling. The systematic&#10;identification of impurities inside plastic ... ## [2]&#10;Identification of Aged Polypropylene with Machine Learning and&#10;Near–Infrared Spectroscopy for Improved Recycling (2025,&#10;venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth = 1&#10;cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 7 section_weight = 0.0&#10;delta_year = unknown is_influential = False low_confidence =&#10;True cited_by_subgraph = 0 [PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[]&#10;delta_yr=unknown edge_type=none [IDEA] Problem: The&#10;traditional plasti...&#10;&#10;Trend Summary:&#10;Trends: Paper 0 to Paper 1: Paper 0, “Detecting Contaminants&#10;in Post-Consumer Plastic Packaging Waste by a NIR&#10;Hyperspectral Imaging-Based Cascade Detection Approach”&#10;(2021), treats plastic-waste management primarily as a&#10;sorting-quality problem: post-consumer packaging must be&#10;cleaned of impurities before mechanical recycling can be&#10;effective. Its main contribution is the use of near-infrared&#10;hyperspectral imaging in a cascade-detection framework to&#10;identify contaminants inside plastic packaging waste. Paper 1,&#10;“Identification of Aged Polypropylene with Machine Learning&#10;and Near-Infrared Spectroscopy for Improved Recycling” (2025),&#10;builds on the same broad premise—optical sensing can make&#10;mixed plastic waste legible—but shifts the target from&#10;contaminant detection to material-state recognition. Instead&#10;of asking only “Is there an impurity?”, it asks “Can we&#10;identify aged polypropylene well enough to improve sorting?”&#10;This is an important advance because polymer aging, surface&#10;condition, and material history can affect recyclability,&#10;meaning that successful recycling depends not only on&#10;separating foreign objects but also on distinguishing usable&#10;from degraded plastic. The transition therefore moves from&#10;impurity screening toward quality-aware plastic&#10;classification. Paper 1 to Paper 2: Paper 2, “Development of&#10;an Economically Viable AI-Enabled Sorting Technology for&#10;Complex Plastic Waste Streams” (2025), expands the scope&#10;again. Where Paper 1 focuses on a specific material class—aged&#10;polypropylene—using machine learning and near-infrared&#10;spectroscopy, Paper 2 frames the challenge as a system-level&#10;sorting problem for complex plastic streams, especially&#10;difficult categories such as #3–#7 plastics. Its advance is&#10;the integration of artificial intelligence with sensor fusion&#10;and an explicit emphasis on economic viability. This matters&#10;because real recycling facilities do not only need accurate&#10;laboratory-style classification; they need technologies that&#10;can operate on heterogeneous, high-throughput waste streams at&#10;a cost that facilities can bear. The progression therefore&#10;moves from single-purpose contaminant detection, to targeted&#10;polymer-condition identification, to a broader AI-enabled&#10;sorting architecture designed for industrially complex waste.&#10;Key Turning Points: The first turning point is the move from&#10;manual or coarse mechanical sorting toward sens..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d9eaf7;strokeColor=#5b9bd5;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="968" width="560" height="2040" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step3_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step3_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step4" value="Step 4 RSS Extraction&#10;status: completed&#10;rss_keys: ['bottlenecks',&#10;'core_gap',&#10;'desired_solution_properties',&#10;'inspiration_search_hints',&#10;'objects',&#10;'possible_mechanism_types',&#10;'relations', 'target_domain',&#10;'unresolved_tensions']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="3108" width="246" height="204" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step3_step_step4" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step_step4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step4_rss_panel" value="Step 4 RSS&#10;target_domain: Data-driven plastic waste management,&#10;especially online characterization, sorting, and material-flow&#10;decision support for mixed, post-consumer plastic streams.&#10;&#10;core_gap: Existing NIR/MIR spectroscopy, machine-learning&#10;classification, and early sensor-fusion sorting systems cannot&#10;yet provide robust, generalizable, uncertainty-aware,&#10;economically deployable characterization of dirty, aged,&#10;contaminated, pigmented, multilayer, and #3–#7 plastic waste&#10;under industrial conditions, nor reliably connect item-level&#10;material-state recognition to material-flow analysis,&#10;recycling yield, and circular-economy decisions.&#10;&#10;objects:&#10;- mixed post-consumer plastic waste streams&#10;- plastic packaging waste&#10;- polymer types and resin categories&#10;- aged or degraded polymer states&#10;- contaminants, residues, labels, inks, additives, and&#10;pigments&#10;- multilayer and mixed-polymer structures&#10;- NIR and MIR spectroscopy&#10;- hyperspectral imaging&#10;- machine-learning classification models&#10;- sensor-fusion sorting systems&#10;- material flow analysis&#10;- recycling facilities and sorting lines&#10;- economic feasibility and environmental impact metrics&#10;- policy interventions such as bottle recycling, bag bans, and&#10;waste-to-fuel use&#10;&#10;relations:&#10;- spectral signatures are used to infer polymer identity,&#10;contamination, and material condition&#10;- contamination, aging, additives, and surface degradation&#10;reduce sorting confidence and recyclability&#10;- sensor fusion combines complementary modalities to improve&#10;discrimination of hard-to-sort plastics&#10;- material-state classification drives sorting, recovery,&#10;downcycling, or diversion decisions&#10;- item-level characterization should aggregate into reliable&#10;material-flow data&#10;- material flow analysis informs economically feasible and&#10;environmentally advantageous waste-management interventions&#10;- throughput, cost, and facility constraints limit the&#10;deployment of sensing and AI models&#10;&#10;bottlenecks:&#10;- spectral signal distortion caused by contamination, aging,&#10;pigments, moisture, labels, inks, fillers, and multilayer&#10;structures&#10;- poor generalization of trained models across waste streams,&#10;facilities, instruments, and geographic contexts&#10;- limited calibration transfer, domain adaptation, and&#10;uncertainty quantification in sorting systems&#10;- difficulty of identifying and separating low-value,&#10;heterogeneous #3–#7 plastics and multilayer packaging&#10;- need for high-throughput, low-cost, low-maintenance&#10;operation in real recycling facilities&#10;- disconnect between sensor-level classification and broader&#10;material-flow, economic, and policy decision-making&#10;&#10;unresolved_tensions:&#10;- high classification accuracy versus high-speed, low-cost&#10;industrial operation&#10;- rich multimodal sensing versus hardware affordability and&#10;maintenance burden&#10;- polymer identification versus assessment of material&#10;condition and recyclability&#10;- local waste-stream specificity versus globally transferable&#10;models&#10;- environmental benefit versus economic feasibility&#10;- detailed material characterization versus actionable&#10;material-flow aggregation&#10;&#10;desired_solution_properties:&#10;- robust to dirty, aged, contaminated, pigmented, multilayer,&#10;and additive-laden plastics&#10;- operable online or inline with minimal sample preparation&#10;- capable of uncertainty estimation and confidence-aware&#10;sorting decisions&#10;- generalizable across instruments, facilities, waste streams,&#10;and geographic contexts&#10;- economically viable for high-throughput recycling operations&#10;- able to handle difficult #3–#7 plastics, mixed polymers, and&#10;low-value streams&#10;- able to connect material-state recognition with material&#10;flow analysis, recycling yield, and circular-economy decisions&#10;- supportive of policy and operational interventions such as&#10;bottle recycling, bag reduction, and waste-to-fuel pathways&#10;&#10;possible_mechanism_types:&#10;- multimodal sensor fusion with confidence-aware decision&#10;making&#10;- domain adaptation and calibration transfer for spectral&#10;models&#10;- physics-informed or chemically informed spectral&#10;interpretation&#10;- hierarchical cascade detection and sorting&#10;- probabilistic material-state estimation&#10;- representation learning for noisy, contaminated, or degraded&#10;plastic signals&#10;- decision-theoretic routing under cost, speed, and hardware&#10;constraints&#10;- integration of sensor telemetry with material-flow&#10;accounting&#10;- techno-economic and environmental optimization loops&#10;&#10;inspiration_search_hints:&#10;- hyperspectral remote sensing and spectral unmixing for mixed&#10;materials&#10;- pharmaceutical process analytical technology and&#10;chemometrics&#10;- food-quality inspection under contamination and natural&#10;variability&#10;- mineral and ore sorting using sensor fusion&#10;- industrial process control with calibration transfer and&#10;drift correction&#10;- medical multimodal triage under uncertainty&#10;- circular-economy industrial symbiosis and waste-to-energy&#10;optimization&#10;- autonomous perception under sensor noise, domain shift, and&#10;deployment constraints" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e2f0d9;strokeColor=#70ad47;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="3108" width="560" height="2418" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step4_rss_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step4_rss_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step5" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;status: completed&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="5626" width="246" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step4_step_step5" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step_step5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step5_gate_panel" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;use_same_field: True&#10;use_cross_domain: True&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;same_field_top_k: 2&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;cross_domain_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;rationale: Same-field retrieval is needed to remain grounded&#10;in plastic-waste spectroscopy, sorting, and recycling&#10;feasibility. Cross-domain retrieval is justified because&#10;structural bottlenecks—contamination-induced signal&#10;distortion, domain shift, uncertainty-aware fusion, and&#10;linkage to material-flow decisions—suggest transferable&#10;mechanisms from fields such as chemometrics, remote sensing,&#10;process control, or multimodal triage." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fce4d6;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="5626" width="560" height="294" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step5_gate_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step5_gate_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step6" value="Step 6 Multi-radius Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;candidate_count: 6&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6020" width="239" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step5_step_step6" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step_step6"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step6_candidates_panel" value="Step 6 Retrieval Candidates&#10;count: 6&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 1&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: An Innovative Route to Circular Rigid Plastics&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: An innovative route for plastics recycling is&#10;proposed, based on a combination of a logarithmic sorting&#10;process and colour plus high-resolution near-infrared (NIR)&#10;sensors. Although counterintuitive, it is shown that such a&#10;technology could sort clean flakes from rigid packagi...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 2&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: NIR-MFCO dataset: Near-infrared-based false-color&#10;images of post-consumer plastics at different material flow&#10;compositions and material flow presentations&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Determining mass-based material flow compositions&#10;(MFCOs) is crucial for assessing and optimizing the recycling&#10;of post-consumer plastics. Currently, MFCOs in plastic&#10;recycling are primarily determined through manual sorting&#10;analysis, but the use of inline near-infrared (NIR)...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 3&#10;domain: remote_sensing&#10;radius: R2&#10;t